WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.380
HAI QI: What is neuroimmunology? it's a&nbsp;
study that the bi-directional influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:12.647
or interaction between these two arguably&nbsp;
most complex physiological system in our body

00:00:12.647 --> 00:00:14.820
VOICE OVER:. Hai Qi studies the connections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.900
and interactions between the&nbsp;
brain and the immune system.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:25.980
HAI: They're both based on a network of&nbsp;
interaction of the cells. One case neuron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:31.380
the other cases you know lymphocytes and&nbsp;
many other immune cells. They both have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:38.340
memory ,they both play a role in defense&nbsp;
and in in reaction to external stimuli&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.520
etcetera, and they cross regulate each other.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.420
VO: But could these interactions&nbsp;
be harnessed for health benefits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.380
can we influence our own&nbsp;
immune systems with our minds.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:01:02.460
HAI: I was a trained as a supposedly clinician&nbsp;
um back in the in the 90s actually last century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:08.940
But then I I didn't go practice medicine but&nbsp;
went to grad school in the United States. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:15.120
became interested in T cell biology. Then&nbsp;
after graduate school I went to National&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:23.700
Institute of Health where we study T cell and B&nbsp;
cell interaction for germ center response. So I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:30.120
became even more interested in the intricacy&nbsp;
of how T cell regulates B cell response.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.800
VO: And since setting up his own lab&nbsp;
Hai has continued to investigate how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.180
these two lymphocytes collaborate with&nbsp;
each other and how these processes are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.320
regulated in the body and this&nbsp;
interest eventually led him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:48.120
neuroimmunology understanding how the&nbsp;
brain influences the immune system.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:57.480
HAI: I, as a child growing up observed that that&nbsp;
many people claim their thinking exercise or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:05.520
Qigong that type of a practice will bring out&nbsp;
a health benefit you know in Chinese there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:13.620
a word we say Miǎnyì lì [免疫力] loosely it means&nbsp;
you know the the, your capability of sort of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:23.760
general defense against the variety of potential&nbsp;
pathogens or insults uh so it has been claimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:30.300
that the mental state would influence that&nbsp;
ability so making me wonder what's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:36.960
connection between our mental states and our&nbsp;
uh with our sort of a physical health state.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:42.720
VO: So high and his colleagues looked to&nbsp;
animal models first attempting to identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.940
and remove nerves that could be providing&nbsp;
the link between brain and immune systems.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:56.340
HAI: With the hypothesis that if we remove a&nbsp;
nerve that potentially transmits a neuronal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:59.760
signal into a lymphoid organ that can&nbsp;
regulate the immune response then we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.960
should see a difference in the immune&nbsp;
response that animal will generate.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:11.580
That's how we started and then initially&nbsp;
we indeed observed that by removing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:19.020
spleen nerve in a mouse model uh then&nbsp;
following a model antigen immunization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:23.576
the antibody response of the mouse was impaired .

00:03:23.576 --> 00:03:28.020
VO: The team found that the plasma&nbsp;
cell response - the cells that produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:33.540
antibodies in response to a vaccine - was&nbsp;
influenced by activity in the spleen nerve.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.890
Removing the nerve reduced production&nbsp;
of plasma cells following immunization.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:44.160
HAI: Then we trace this neuronal signal&nbsp;
carried by the spleen nerve upstream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:51.000
then we we find that there are at least a two&nbsp;
important brain regions in the central nervous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:57.480
system that are important for this spleen nerve&nbsp;
dependent promotion of plasma cell formation.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:02.700
One is called amygdala the other&nbsp;
is paraventricular nucleus.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.900
VO: These regions can respond&nbsp;
to certain behaviors or stimuli.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:11.520
For the mice in the experiment this&nbsp;
was mild stress, they were placed on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.240
a transparent platform too high&nbsp;
for them to happily jump off of.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:19.740
HAI: Under that mild stress in this&nbsp;
two regions there are special subsets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:28.740
neurons called a CRS neuron will be activated&nbsp;
and these neurons and their activation cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:38.940
a signal in the spleen nerve that transmits into&nbsp;
the spleen and that signal is presumably read by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:47.760
a special subset of T cells and that T cells will&nbsp;
convert that nerve signal then relay that signal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:55.920
to to B cells and that signal promote these&nbsp;
B cells to become and expand as plasma cells.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:02.280
VO: They had found a route for a behavioral&nbsp;
stimuli to modulate an immune response in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:08.100
mice and that opens the door to some fascinating&nbsp;
possibilities if it can be replicated in humans.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:13.020
Hai thinks this link could offer a new&nbsp;
target for drugs aimed at manipulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:17.160
immune response but this isn't the&nbsp;
only outcome he's interested in.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:23.700
HAI: One can start to imagine that at the&nbsp;
conscious level that that we might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:29.040
certain brain exercise that will influence&nbsp;
the function of the limbic system then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:38.280
subsequently influenced how our immune system&nbsp;
operate. It seems to become possible that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:48.060
one day we could design a thinking exercise&nbsp;
that actually boosts your antibody response.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:54.540
Potentially you can have a a&nbsp;
central nervous system memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.780
that can have an impact on&nbsp;
a memory immune response.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:07.980
Yeah I find that's a that's a very&nbsp;
um intriguing and and exciting.

